From 3ff671f5b2e7d57387c7243978364bf1ec22fa8e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael Catanzaro <mcatanzaro@redhat.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Jan 2026 11:59:53 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 06/50] Remove support for rehandshaking

For 6 years now, since glib!1305, our documentation has indicated that
initiating a rehandshake is no longer supported and does nothing. (The
only other way to rehandshake would be if the peer requests it, but
glib-networking has always ignored such requests.)

Although I updated the documentation to indicate that rehandshaking no
longer does anything, I apparently only removed the code only on a side
branch that never landed, then forgot about the problem. So actually,
rehandshaking is still supported, provided TLS 1.2 is in use. This is
not very useful because there is no way to check whether TLS 1.2 is in
use.

The rehandshake tests are now failing for OpenSSL when newer OpenSSL
verisons are used, and when testing TLS 1.3, because glib-networking
fails the rehandshake if the peer does not support safe renegotiation,
and OpenSSL has stopped advertising safe renegotiation in TLS 1.3, which
is fine because renegotiation does not exist anymore in TLS 1.3.

This seems like a good opportunity to simplify our code and tests. I
would be very surprised if any application using GTlsConnection actually
intentionally performs rehandshakes, so it should be safe to remove.

I thought about keeping the tests in order to test that nothing happens
if a rehandshake is attempted, but that's simply not worth the added
complexity.

Fixes #222

Part-of: <https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/glib-networking/-/merge_requests/267>
